# NEMO Seamless Gutter — daily growth engine.
#
# Installed at /etc/cron.d/nemo-growth. This droplet keeps per-project crons in
# /etc/cron.d/, NOT in root's user crontab — never pipe to `crontab -` here, it
# clobbers the other sites' jobs.
#
# 06:00 America/New_York: measure yesterday, judge what's running, build today's
# page, tell search engines, scout for new techniques, and report.
#
# CRON_TZ matters: the droplet runs UTC, and without it the job would drift an
# hour every daylight-saving change and stop being "6am" to the business.
#
# Credentials are NOT sourced here — growth_daily.py reads seo/.env and
# server/.env itself, because server/.env holds unquoted values with spaces
# that a shell `.` would try to execute.
CRON_TZ=America/New_York
SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin

# The publish step pushes the metrics snapshot, the journal and any pages
# written this morning to GitHub. The daily review agent runs in Anthropic's
# cloud and cannot reach this droplet, so the repository is how it sees the
# numbers. It runs even if the engine failed — a snapshot showing a failed run
# is exactly what the agent needs in order to notice.
#
# Two reports go out, because they have different readers. --email is the
# developer's copy (build log, scout proposals, technique slugs, log paths).
# --owner-email is Eric's: leads, rank, what is new on his site and what needs
# him, with none of the machinery — a report full of internals is a report
# that stops getting read.
#
# The developer's copy went from daily to WEEKLY on 2026-08-27, at the owner's
# request. Eric's copy is untouched and still goes every morning.
#
# It is done by splitting the SAME 6am entry on the day-of-week field rather
# than by adding a second job that re-runs `report`, because the report is built
# from the run that just happened. A separate Friday entry would either collide
# with the 06:00 run or read a ledger it was still writing, and would show up in
# the inbox as a report about a run that had not finished. Two lines that differ
# only by `--email` is the duplication that buys that.
#
# 0-4,6 is Sun-Thu and Sat; 5 is Friday. Both lines must be kept in step — if
# you change one, change the other.

# Sun-Thu, Sat — Eric's report only.
0 6 * * 0-4,6 root cd /var/www/nemo-seamless-gutter && WEB_ROOT=/var/www/nemo-seamless-gutter /usr/bin/python3 growth_daily.py daily --owner-email eric@nemoseamlessgutter.com >> /var/log/nemo-growth.log 2>&1; cd /var/www/nemo-seamless-gutter && WEB_ROOT=/var/www/nemo-seamless-gutter /bin/bash growth/publish_state.sh >> /var/log/nemo-growth.log 2>&1

# Friday — the same run, plus the developer's weekly copy.
0 6 * * 5 root cd /var/www/nemo-seamless-gutter && WEB_ROOT=/var/www/nemo-seamless-gutter /usr/bin/python3 growth_daily.py daily --email divinejdavis@gmail.com --owner-email eric@nemoseamlessgutter.com >> /var/log/nemo-growth.log 2>&1; cd /var/www/nemo-seamless-gutter && WEB_ROOT=/var/www/nemo-seamless-gutter /bin/bash growth/publish_state.sh >> /var/log/nemo-growth.log 2>&1

# Watchdog — runs from its own entry so it survives the engine failing. If the
# 06:00 run stops happening, the failure mode is an empty inbox, which looks
# exactly like a quiet week. This makes silence loud.
#
# Stays DAILY through the weekly-report change: cmd_watchdog sends nothing when
# it finds nothing wrong, so this is not a daily email — it is the alarm. Moving
# it to Fridays would mean a Saturday breakage went unreported for six days,
# which is the exact failure the entry exists to catch.
0 11 * * * root cd /var/www/nemo-seamless-gutter && WEB_ROOT=/var/www/nemo-seamless-gutter /usr/bin/python3 growth_daily.py watchdog --email divinejdavis@gmail.com >> /var/log/nemo-growth.log 2>&1
